WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:03.860
Templates and expression evaluators are interpreters.

2
00:00:03.960 --> 00:00:18.040
C# applications may use Razor views, mail templates, reporting templates, rules engines, expression evaluators, dynamic scripting, reflection-based plugins, or runtime compilation features.

3
00:00:18.140 --> 00:00:22.380
These mechanisms are useful because they turn structured instructions into behavior.

4
00:00:22.480 --> 00:00:32.400
They become risky when user-controlled input can influence template logic, expression text, code paths, type names, method names, or evaluation context.

5
00:00:32.680 --> 00:00:43.800
Razor and ASP.NET Core provide strong default encoding for ordinary content, but developers can bypass those protections with raw-output features or custom helpers.

6
00:00:43.900 --> 00:00:51.340
The issue is not that Razor is unsafe; the issue is treating user content as if it were trusted template logic or markup.

7
00:00:51.440 --> 00:01:02.880
A support message, product description, profile field, or imported text should render as content unless there is a deliberate, reviewed, and constrained reason to allow richer behavior.

8
00:01:03.160 --> 00:01:07.480
Expression evaluators and rule engines need clear trust boundaries.

9
00:01:07.580 --> 00:01:13.660
Administrator-authored rules may be acceptable in some products, while normal end-user expressions are not.

10
00:01:13.760 --> 00:01:21.200
Sandboxes and restricted execution modes can reduce risk, but they should not be treated as permission to evaluate arbitrary input.

11
00:01:21.300 --> 00:01:32.580
Review dependencies that interpret strings, build expressions dynamically, compile code, call reflection APIs, or load plugin assemblies based on external values.

12
00:01:32.860 --> 00:01:37.340
A defensible pattern separates user content from executable logic.

13
00:01:37.440 --> 00:01:44.000
Store templates in trusted locations, map user choices to known templates, and pass user values as data.

14
00:01:44.100 --> 00:01:50.580
Avoid letting requests select template bodies, expression bodies, type names, or method calls.

15
00:01:50.680 --> 00:01:59.400
If dynamic behavior is a product requirement, design a small policy language with explicit operations and tests instead of exposing a general-purpose interpreter.

16
00:01:59.500 --> 00:02:03.100
The goal is controlled flexibility, not open-ended evaluation.
